870
00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:35.100
I don't know where they found high heels my size, but it was.
我不知道他们在哪找到我这个尺码的高跟鞋，但它……

871
00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:35.760
What's your shoe size?
你穿几码的鞋？

872
00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:38.100
11 and a half, a modest 11 and a half.
11号半，谦虚的11号半。

873
00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:44.680
We're not, you know, but it was, I don't know if you guys know this, extremely hard to walk in.
我们没有，你知道的，但它——我不知道你们知不知道这事——穿起来极其难走路。

874
00:39:45.880 --> 00:39:47.000
We have an idea.
我们大概能体会到。

875
00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:47.380
What?
什么？

876
00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:48.080
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

877
00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:48.920
I know.
我知道。

878
00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:50.980
No man has ever told you this.
从没有哪个男人跟你说过这事。

879
00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:54.260
It was so hard to move.
移动起来太难了。

880
00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:55.080
I couldn't believe it.
我简直不敢相信。

881
00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:57.620
I was like, wait, people do this for hours at a time?
我当时想，等等，人们竟然能连续几个小时这样走路？

882
00:39:57.800 --> 00:39:58.800
It's kind of insane.
这简直有点疯狂。

883
00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.760
It's ridiculous when you think about it.
仔细想想这太荒谬了。

884
00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:03.360
It's like, I'm going to design a shoe where you're up on your tippy toes.
就像是，我要设计一种让你得踮着脚尖走路的鞋。

885
00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:04.480
Now go walk all day.
现在去走上一整天吧。

886
00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:05.520
Not just that.
不仅如此。

887
00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:06.680
So many high heels.
有那么多高跟鞋。

888
00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:10.500
They're made for a foot where you just have one long center toe.
它们的设计是给那种只有一根长长中间脚趾的脚穿的。

889
00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:11.640
Yeah, it's true.
对，确实如此。

890
00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:13.540
It's like not even the shape of your foot.
这甚至都不是你脚的形状。

891
00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:14.480
Which luckily I do have.
好在我确实有这种脚型。

892
00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:14.980
That is all.
仅此而已。

893
00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:16.380
I just have a long center toe.
我只有一根长长的中间脚趾。

894
00:40:16.380 --> 00:40:17.900
That was also an Abraham Lincoln.
这也是亚伯拉罕·林肯的特征。

895
00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:20.400
Yes, that's one of the symptoms of Marfan syndrome.
是的，那是马凡氏综合征的症状之一。

896
00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:22.620
Just a single long toe.
只有一根长脚趾。

897
00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:28.140
In terms of volume, it's the same volume as five toes, but just in length.
就体积而言，它和五根脚趾的体积一样，只不过长了一点。

898
00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:28.640
In length.
在长度上。

899
00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:29.100
In the one toe.
长在这一根脚趾上。

900
00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:29.800
Yes, exactly.
是的，完全正确。

901
00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:30.760
All right.
好的。

902
00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.380
We have one other question we've been curious about.
我们还有另一个一直很好奇的问题。

903
00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:33.660
Great.
太好了。

904
00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:35.060
The Great Danes.
大丹犬。

905
00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:37.760
You had to work a lot with these giant dogs.
你们得跟这些巨型犬有很多合作。

906
00:40:38.140 --> 00:40:41.740
Was there anything, like any memory from working with those dogs?
有什么跟那些狗合作的记忆吗？

907
00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:44.400
Yeah, they were like frustratingly professional.
对，它们专业得让人有点沮丧。

908
00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:44.940
Really?
真的吗？

909
00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:45.460
Like, yeah.
对啊。

910
00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:49.340
I was like, you know, with a dog, you just want to like rub your face in their snout.
我就觉得吧，你知道的，对狗，你只想把脸埋在它们的鼻口上蹭蹭。

911
00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:50.940
You just want to be like, oh, I love you.
你只想说，哦，我爱你。

912
00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:51.540
You're beautiful.
你好漂亮。

913
00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:52.200
You're a miracle.
你是个奇迹。

914
00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:54.280
And they were just like, we're working.
而它们却一副我们在工作的样子。

915
00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.320
They just were so focused.
它们实在是太专注了。

916
00:40:56.480 --> 00:41:00.060
They had that like working dog thing where they were just like not screwing around.
它们有那种工作犬的特质，就好像完全不是在开玩笑。

917
00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:00.500
Yeah.
是啊。

918
00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:01.100
And I was like.
然后我就觉得。

919
00:41:01.180 --> 00:41:01.880
No snuggles.
不给抱抱。

920
00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:02.660
No snuggles.
不给抱抱。

921
00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:04.820
I was like, spoon me, Great Danes.
我当时就想说，来抱抱我吧，大丹犬。

922
00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:06.260
And they're like, no, thank you.
而它们却说，不用了，谢谢。

923
00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:08.440
No, thank you, strange human.
不用了，谢谢，奇怪的人类。

924
00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:10.180
I'm working.
我在工作呢。

925
00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:10.680
Yeah.
是啊。

926
00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:14.980
I was like, it truly was like, they were like, like, you ever meet like one of those former
我就觉得，真的就像是，它们就像是，你有没有见过那种以前的

927
00:41:14.980 --> 00:41:17.780
child stars who's been like working from the time they were three and they're like,
童星，从三岁起就开始工作的那种，他们会说，

928
00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:18.300
where's my light?
我的光在哪？

929
00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:21.040
OK, I just need a little bit of eyebrow pencil and let's go.
好吧，我只需要一点眉笔，然后我们开始吧。

930
00:41:21.300 --> 00:41:25.180
You know, there's people who are just like they were like that, but just the canine version.
你知道的，有这样的人，它们就像那样，只不过是犬类版本。

931
00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:25.520
Yeah.
是啊。

932
00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:27.880
Do you share Gabe's love of horror films?
你也像盖比一样喜欢恐怖片吗？

933
00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:28.760
No.
不。

934
00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:31.180
I mean, I like some horror movies, but not really.
我是说，我喜欢一些恐怖电影，但其实也没有很喜欢。

935
00:41:31.360 --> 00:41:34.440
What I do love is I really like wake up pranks.
我真正喜欢的是，我真的很喜欢恶作剧叫醒别人。

936
00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:35.860
Do you guys know about wake up pranks?
你们知道叫醒恶作剧吗？

937
00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:36.520
No.
不知道。

938
00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:38.360
Do you wake up someone in a horrible manner?
是用很可怕的方式把别人叫醒吗？

939
00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:38.680
Yeah.
对。

940
00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:40.680
It's like people waking up their friends in mean ways.
就像人们用恶劣的方式叫醒他们的朋友。

941
00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:41.340
Oh, no.
哦，不。

942
00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:42.120
And people get scared.
然后人们会被吓到。

943
00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:44.360
And I think it's very funny and very mean.
我觉得这非常搞笑，也非常恶劣。

944
00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:47.500
And there's one where they're like, this guy's asleep.
其中有一个是这样的，这家伙睡着了。

945
00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:48.740
I guess they got like fast food.
我猜他们买了快餐之类的。

946
00:41:48.860 --> 00:41:49.460
They're in a van.
他们在面包车里。

947
00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:50.240
This guy's asleep.
这家伙睡着了。

948
00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:53.700
He's got like a burger on his lap, but he fell asleep before he ate his burger.
他的大腿上放着一个汉堡，但他还没吃汉堡就睡着了。

949
00:41:54.160 --> 00:41:57.740
And I think there's like a truck with one of those.
然后我觉得好像有一辆卡车带着那种。

950
00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.820
It's like one of those trucks that pulls cars behind it.
就像那种后面拖着汽车的卡车。

951
00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.900
So it looks like the car is facing.
所以看起来这辆车正对着。

952
00:42:03.320 --> 00:42:03.840
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

953
00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:04.740
Like the vehicle.
就像那辆车一样。

954
00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:07.420
You can mistake it as an oncoming vehicle.
你可能会把它误认为是迎面开来的车。

955
00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:07.720
Right.
对。

956
00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:07.920
Right.
没错。

957
00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:09.380
So this guy's asleep.
所以这家伙睡着了。

958
00:42:09.580 --> 00:42:11.400
So everyone in the car decides to wake up prank him.
车里的人就决定恶作剧叫醒他。

959
00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:15.500
And what they do is they swerve and scream all at the same time.
他们做的是同时猛打方向盘并尖叫。

960
00:42:15.500 --> 00:42:18.560
And he wakes up and he sees this car in front of him.
然后他醒了，看到前面有这辆车。

961
00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:20.100
And he thinks, you know, this is it.
他心想，你知道的，这下完蛋了。

962
00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:24.060
And he screams and he just squeezes the hell out of his burger.
然后他尖叫起来，死命地捏着他的汉堡。

963
00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:27.200
He just squeezes this burger so hard.
他把这个汉堡捏得死死的。

964
00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:29.160
And then they show it in slow-mo.
然后他们用慢动作播放出来。

965
00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:30.740
It's just like face contorting.
简直就是面部扭曲。

966
00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:32.100
And I'm squeezing the burger.
而我正在捏着汉堡。

967
00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:33.480
It's so fun.
太好玩了。

968
00:42:33.620 --> 00:42:34.420
It's very mean.
这太恶劣了。

969
00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:35.400
And I don't think it's right.
而且我觉得这不太对。

970
00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:36.260
But it is very funny.
但确实非常好笑。

971
00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:42.100
And then I also like close call videos where it's like I can't stand like fail videos where people get hurt.
然后我也喜欢那种千钧一发的视频，就是我受不了那种有人受伤的搞笑翻车视频。

972
00:42:42.100 --> 00:42:43.280
I can't stand it.
我实在受不了。

973
00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:44.980
But close call videos are the beSt.
但千钧一发的视频是最棒的。

974
00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:45.820
Oh, and someone almost falls?
哦，有人差点摔倒吗？

975
00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:46.180
Yes.
是的。

976
00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:46.580
Yes.
没错。

977
00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:47.340
Yes.
对。

978
00:42:47.480 --> 00:42:48.360
It's the beSt.
这太棒了。

979
00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:49.000
It's the beSt.
太棒了。

980
00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:50.620
Like I haven't seen the videos.
虽然我还没看过这些视频。

981
00:42:50.620 --> 00:42:53.600
But I will tell you, I have a memory I will never forget.
但我得告诉你，我有一段永远不会忘记的记忆。

982
00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:55.680
I was in the Dallas-Fort Worth airport.
我在达拉斯-沃斯堡机场。

983
00:42:56.300 --> 00:43:00.280
And there was this long corridor that we were all walking down.
有一条长长的走廊，我们都在那上面走着。

984
00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:00.680
Right.
对。

985
00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.880
And there's a woman coming kind of swimming upstream against all of us.
有个女人正逆着我们所有人的人流往前走。

986
00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:11.940
She was clearly very late.
她很明显快迟到了。

987
00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:12.620
Wait for her flight.
等她的航班。

988
00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:12.960
Okay.
好的。

989
00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:14.660
And she is running.
她在跑。

990
00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:18.840
I'm talking a full sprint with like trying to juggle a carry-on.
我是说全力冲刺的那种，还一边试着拿随身行李。

991
00:43:18.940 --> 00:43:19.940
But a full sprint.
真的是全力冲刺。

992
00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:22.080
And she's in wedge flip-flops.
而且她脚上还穿着坡跟人字拖。

993
00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:23.680
And she is running.
她就在那跑着。

994
00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:26.840
And we can see her from so far in this corridor.
我们在走廊很远的地方就能看到她。

995
00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:27.480
And she's running.
她一直在跑。

996
00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:31.360
And the front of her flip-flop bends forward under her foot.
然后她人字拖的前端在脚下向前弯折。

997
00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:34.180
And then she lurches forward.
接着她身体猛地向前一冲。

998
00:43:34.360 --> 00:43:36.660
And then she tries to overcompensate.
然后她试图用力调整平衡。

999
00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:37.840
And she lurches back.
结果身体又猛地向后仰。

1000
00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:43.880
And she did it for probably, it felt like, 10 minutes.
这种情况大概持续了——感觉就像有10分钟那么久。

1001
00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.300
It was like, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.
当时就像是，等等，等等，等等，等等，等等，等等。

1002
00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:48.580
And we were all so invested.
而且我们都投入了极大的关注。

1003
00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:51.500
It was like, I don't know, 80 strangers.
当时大概有，我不知道，80个陌生人吧。

1004
00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:52.720
And we're like, is she going down?
我们都在想，她要摔下去了吗？

1005
00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:53.560
What do we do?
我们该怎么办？

1006
00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:58.140
And then she, right before she face-planted, she pulled herself up.
然后她，就在她快要脸朝下摔倒之前，把自己拉了上来。

1007
00:43:58.280 --> 00:43:58.740
Yeah.
对。

1008
00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:00.520
And we were all like, ah!
然后我们都喊道：啊！

1009
00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:03.060
And then she ran past us.
接着她从我们身边跑了过去。

1010
00:44:03.480 --> 00:44:05.200
I'll never forget it.
我永远不会忘记那一幕。

1011
00:44:05.540 --> 00:44:06.480
Oh, my God.
噢，天哪。

1012
00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:07.580
I've seen videos.
我看过那些视频。

1013
00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:10.880
Have you seen the videos of someone who's like slipping on ice?
你看过那种有人在冰上滑倒的视频吗？

1014
00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:11.110
Yes.
看过。

1015
00:44:11.110 --> 00:44:13.220
But for like a very long time.
但那种状态持续了非常长的时间。

1016
00:44:13.620 --> 00:44:13.920
Yes.
对。

1017
00:44:14.420 --> 00:44:15.400
It's like that.
就像那样。

1018
00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:16.240
It's like that flip-flop lady.
就像那个穿人字拖的女士。

1019
00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:16.820
Yes.
是的。

1020
00:44:16.820 --> 00:44:22.340
Like that's what it feels like to be a person is to be just like a prolonged ice slip that seems to never stop.
这就好像做人的人生体验一样，就像是一次仿佛永远不会结束的漫长滑冰摔倒。

1021
00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:24.180
And when it turns out good, right?
而且当结果是好的时候，对吧？

1022
00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:25.840
I'm so relieved.
我真是松了一口气。

1023
00:44:26.080 --> 00:44:28.320
And it, but there's so many, and you can watch them in compilations.
而且，这种视频有好多，你可以在集锦里看。

1024
00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:33.940
It's like people like their kid almost like is riding a bike and almost runs into something and they swoop in and grab them.
就像人们的效仿，比如他们的孩子差点骑着自行车撞到什么东西，然后他们猛地冲过去抓住孩子。

1025
00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:38.600
Or it's like, it's just like humans evading disaster is like the beSt.
或者就像是，人类躲过灾难简直是最棒的事。

1026
00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:44.020
Have you seen the videos of people who they're scaring someone?
你看过那种整蛊别人的视频吗？

1027
00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:44.580
Yes.
看过。

1028
00:44:44.760 --> 00:44:49.160
But they're doing it by pretending that they're scared of something themselves?
但他们整蛊的方式是假装自己看到了什么可怕的东西？

1029
00:44:49.400 --> 00:44:49.540
Yes.
对。

1030
00:44:49.540 --> 00:44:51.260
And then the other person runs away.
然后另一个人就吓跑了。

1031
00:44:51.400 --> 00:44:51.660
Yes.
是的。

1032
00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:52.460
They're brilliant.
这些太精彩了。

1033
00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:59.040
So like there's like a woman and she's like taking out the trash with her husband and she opens up the trash can and then she's like, ah!
比如有个女人和她丈夫一起去倒垃圾，她打开垃圾桶盖，然后大喊：啊！

1034
00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:03.680
And then he loses, he hasn't even seen anything.
然后他就慌了，他其实什么都没看到。

1035
00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:07.300
And then just like the fight or flight response.
这纯粹是出于战斗或逃跑的本能反应。

1036
00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:13.960
But it's so, but the psychology behind it to me is so interesting because it's like, it's like I share fear with you.
但我觉得背后的心理学太有意思了，因为这就像是我把恐惧分享给了你。

1037
00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:14.660
Yeah.
对。

1038
00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:15.480
We share that.
我们共享了这种恐惧。

1039
00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:19.700
But the one person that's just pretending and then the other person.
但其中一个人只是在装，而另一个人信以为真。

1040
00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:20.680
I know.
我知道。

1041
00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:22.040
It's really fascinating.
真的很迷人。

1042
00:45:22.200 --> 00:45:23.520
I kind of want to try it out.
我有点想试一试。

1043
00:45:23.640 --> 00:45:24.260
You should try it.
你应该试试。

1044
00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:25.680
It's a, it's a cheap prank.
这是一个，这是一个成本很低的恶作剧。

1045
00:45:25.820 --> 00:45:27.120
All you have to do is act scared.
你只需要装得很害怕就行。

1046
00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:30.160
There's one more I have to say because these are now fascinating me.
我还有一个必须讲，因为这些现在让我太着迷了。

1047
00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:31.040
You might like it.
你可能会喜欢。

1048
00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:41.020
So there's the other one where you're in a group with your family and you pick someone and you say, like, I learned we're going to do a magic trick.
还有一个是这样的，你和家人聚在一起，挑中一个人，然后对他说，我学了一个魔术。

1049
00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:50.880
I'm going to make you visible and you put a sheet over them and then you like say some magic words and then you pull the sheet off and everyone in the room has agreed to pretend that the person is invisible.
我要让你隐形，接着你把一张床单盖在他们身上，然后念几句魔术咒语，再把床单扯下来，房间里的所有人都已经串通好，假装那个人是隐形的。

1050
00:45:51.340 --> 00:45:52.400
Oh, that's amazing.
噢，这太绝了。

1051
00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:53.740
It's always the mom.
总是选妈妈。

1052
00:45:53.920 --> 00:45:55.260
They always do it to the mom.
他们总是对妈妈下手。

1053
00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:55.520
Right.
就是说啊。

1054
00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:59.920
And then, so then they pull the sheet off and then everyone's like, no way.
然后，接下来他们会掀开床单，接着所有人都一副“不会吧”的表情。

1055
00:46:00.780 --> 00:46:01.180
Holy.
天哪。

1056
00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:02.280
Oh my God.
我的天啊。

1057
00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:03.360
And then the person's like, what?
然后那个人就问，怎么了？

1058
00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:04.020
Oh my God.
我的天啊。

1059
00:46:04.260 --> 00:46:04.400
What?
什么？

1060
00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:04.900
What?
什么？

1061
00:46:05.140 --> 00:46:05.360
What?
什么？

1062
00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:05.800
You can't.
你不能这样。

1063
00:46:05.900 --> 00:46:06.600
I don't get it.
我搞不懂。

1064
00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:07.680
That's amazing.
太神奇了。

1065
00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:10.920
Also, the fact that it's the mom, like, like an invisible mom.
而且，重点是主角竟然是妈妈，就好像……就好像一个隐形妈妈一样。

1066
00:46:11.100 --> 00:46:14.480
I feel like so many moms in family dynamics are just the invisible caretaker.
我觉得在家庭关系中，好多妈妈都只是隐形的照顾者。

1067
00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:15.000
They're already.
她们本来就……

1068
00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:16.360
They're already invisible.
她们本来就是隐形的。

1069
00:46:16.580 --> 00:46:18.000
It's like that SNL sketch.
就像那个周六夜现场的小品一样。

1070
00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:19.900
That SNL sketch at Christmas.
圣诞节那一期周六夜现场的小品。

1071
00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:22.720
Everyone's saying, I got a piano.
每个人都在说，我得到了一架钢琴。

1072
00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:24.800
And the mom goes, and I got a robe.
然后妈妈说，我得到了一件长袍。

1073
00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:26.200
And that's all she got.
而她就得到了这些。

1074
00:46:26.260 --> 00:46:27.660
And it's like over and over.
而且感觉没完没了。

1075
00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:29.040
But then I got a car.
但随后我说我得到了一辆车。

1076
00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:30.320
I got a robe.
我得到了一件长袍。

1077
00:46:30.320 --> 00:46:33.340
Also, at the end, they're like, wait a second.
还有，最后他们说，等一下。

1078
00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:35.800
What's this secret pile of presents?
这一堆神秘的礼物是什么？

1079
00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:37.300
And the mom's like, oh.
然后妈妈说，哦。

1080
00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:39.880
And they're like, it's presents for the dog.
接着他们说，这是给狗准备的礼物。

1081
00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:43.280
And then the dog gets a robe as well.
然后狗狗也得到了一件长袍。

1082
00:46:43.660 --> 00:46:48.060
I think I'm going to watch the one of, particularly the one where they're scared, where they act scared.
我想我要去看那个，特别是他们假装害怕、表现得很害怕的那一个。

1083
00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:48.660
Yes.
没错。

1084
00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:49.720
It's very fun.
非常有意思。

1085
00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:50.960
I cannot wait.
我等不及了。

1086
00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:52.080
It's so interesting.
太有趣了。

1087
00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:55.700
It's so interesting that people, like, share fear.
人们竟然会分享恐惧，这真的太有趣了。

1088
00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:56.940
It's fascinating.
太迷人了。

1089
00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.660
I heard that, I don't even know if this is true.
我听说过这个，我甚至不知道这是不是真的。

1090
00:46:58.660 --> 00:47:01.220
This could just be something, like, I am basically making up.
这可能只是我基本上凭空捏造出来的东西。

1091
00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:11.040
But that, like, laughter, like, the origins of laughter is it was, like, a way that, like, monkeys could tell each other that a perceived danger was no longer a threat.
但据说，笑声……笑声的起源是，它就像是猴子们用来告诉彼此“察觉到的危险已经不再是威胁”的一种方式。

1092
00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:13.740
Like, so basically, it's like, okay, we're a bunch of monkeys.
所以基本上就是，好吧，我们是一群猴子。

1093
00:47:13.960 --> 00:47:15.360
Like, we think we see a tiger.
比如，我们以为我们看到了一只老虎。

1094
00:47:15.560 --> 00:47:16.660
Everyone gets tense.
所有人都紧张起来。

1095
00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:18.700
And then I realized, oh, it's not a tiger.
然后我意识到，哦，那不是老虎。

1096
00:47:18.860 --> 00:47:20.440
It's something that looked like a tiger, but it isn't a tiger.
那是某种看起来像老虎但并不是老虎的东西。

1097
00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:21.380
Then we all laugh.
然后我们都笑了。

1098
00:47:21.380 --> 00:47:26.400
And it's a way of quickly dispersing the information that, like, what seemed to be threatening is, in fact, not a threat.
这是一种快速传播信息的方式，表明看似具有威胁性的东西实际上并不是威胁。

1099
00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:29.560
So it's, like, this rolling sound that, like, lets everyone know.
所以它就像是一种连绵的声音，让每个人都知道。

1100
00:47:29.620 --> 00:47:30.760
It's like a fire drill in reverse.
这就像是一场反向的消防演习。

1101
00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:32.380
It makes so much sense.
这太合乎逻辑了。

1102
00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:32.700
Right.
对。

1103
00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:40.360
And then also, it makes so much sense why comedy is something we're drawn to watch and that, like, soothes us.
而且这也解释了为什么喜剧是我们爱看、而且能抚慰我们的东西。

1104
00:47:40.540 --> 00:47:41.120
It's a relief.
这是一种解脱。

1105
00:47:41.120 --> 00:47:41.940
Oh, that's interesting.
哦，真有意思。

1106
00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:42.340
Yes.
是的。

1107
00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:50.340
Yes, I mean, like, I remember when I injured my back, when I broke my back, and I watched the Larry Sanders show.
对，我的意思是，就像我记得我背部受伤、背骨骨折的时候，我在看《拉里·桑德斯秀》。

1108
00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:55.560
Me and Creed and Jenna one night in her hotel room binged the Larry Sanders show.
有一天晚上，我和克里德还有珍娜在她酒店房间里疯狂刷了《拉里·桑德斯秀》。

1109
00:47:55.720 --> 00:47:58.640
And, like, that laughter, like, it brings you relief.
然后那种笑声，会带给你一种解脱。

1110
00:47:58.920 --> 00:47:59.100
Yeah.
对。

1111
00:47:59.300 --> 00:47:59.560
Right?
对吧？

1112
00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:00.280
Yes.
是的。

1113
00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:01.880
And maybe it goes all the way back to that.
也许这一切都要追溯到那个时候。

1114
00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:03.100
Just a bunch of monkeys.
只是一群猴子。

1115
00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:04.640
Releasing some.
释放一些压力。

1116
00:48:05.020 --> 00:48:06.080
There's no tiger.
没有老虎。

1117
00:48:06.460 --> 00:48:06.740
Yeah.
是啊。

1118
00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:07.080
Yeah.
对。

1119
00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:13.300
That's our whole show, is just, I'm very scared of what Michael Scott is going to say or do right now.
这就是我们整个节目的核心，就是我很害怕迈克尔·斯科特现在会说出什么或做出什么。

1120
00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:15.020
Okay, all right, all right, all right, all right.
行，好吧，好吧，好吧，好吧。

1121
00:48:15.020 --> 00:48:15.560
I think that's right.
我觉得对。

1122
00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:17.600
It's, like, tension, cringe, release.
这就好比，铺垫、尴尬、释放。

1123
00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:18.400
Yeah, okay, we're fine.
对，好吧，我们没事。

1124
00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:20.280
You know, set up, tension, punchline.
你知道的，蓄势、张力、笑点。

1125
00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:24.320
Like, yeah, I don't know if, again, that could be total malarkey, but.
就是，对啊，我不知道，这也许完全是胡扯，但是。

1126
00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:25.420
I mean, it tracks.
我的意思是，这说得通。

1127
00:48:25.640 --> 00:48:30.940
I've been really into, there's a show about animals on Netflix, and we watched the one about dogs.
我最近特别迷一部网飞上的动物纪录片，我们看了讲狗的那集。

1128
00:48:31.140 --> 00:48:32.060
It's so good.
太棒了。

1129
00:48:32.540 --> 00:48:32.840
Yeah.
对。

1130
00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:37.820
Yeah, and dogs do this little sniffy, snorty thing when they're, you know how they play fight?
对，而且狗在玩耍时会发出那种哼哧哼哧的声音，你知道它们怎么打闹玩的吗？

1131
00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:38.160
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1132
00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:40.520
And it's to let the other dog know that I'm not serious.
这是为了让另一只狗知道我不是认真的。

1133
00:48:40.700 --> 00:48:45.660
And they, so if you hear your dog, and now my little two Chihuahua rescues, when they play, I hear it.
然后它们，所以如果你听到你的狗，现在我那两只吉娃娃救援犬，它们玩的时候，我能听到。

1134
00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:45.880
They go.
它们就这样。

1135
00:48:48.100 --> 00:48:48.420
Really?
真的吗？

1136
00:48:48.560 --> 00:48:51.100
And it's like, it's like, I'm, I'm, I'm just having fun.
这就好像是，我只是在闹着玩。

1137
00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:58.020
That must be so hard on dogs who, like, want to be aggressive but have allergies where they're, like, trying to, like, really, like, big dog another dog.
那对那些想表现得有攻击性却有鼻炎的狗来说一定很难吧，就是它们想装作很凶的样子去压制另一只狗。

1138
00:48:58.020 --> 00:49:03.840
So, again, they're just, like, yeah, like a pug, like a pug can never be taken seriously when he's trying to, like, like, like, be aggressive.
所以，话说回来，就像哈巴狗一样，哈巴狗想表现得有攻击性时，永远不会被人当真。

1139
00:49:03.980 --> 00:49:04.720
Yeah, he's like, I mean it.
对，它就像在说，我是认真的。

1140
00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:05.440
I'm actually angry.
我真的很生气。

1141
00:49:05.640 --> 00:49:06.900
I really mean it, guys.
伙计们，我是认真的。

1142
00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:08.440
Like, no, you don't.
才怪，你才不认真。

1143
00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:09.080
No, you don't.
才怪。

1144
00:49:09.100 --> 00:49:09.860
You're being silly.
你只是在耍宝。

1145
00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:11.280
That's funny.
真搞笑。

1146
00:49:12.300 --> 00:49:14.120
Do you get recognized as Gabe?
你走在街上会被认出来是盖比吗？

1147
00:49:15.100 --> 00:49:15.920
Sometimes, yeah.
有时会的。

1148
00:49:16.220 --> 00:49:17.000
It happens sometimes.
偶尔会发生。

1149
00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:17.860
And what, what is that like?
那，那是一种什么样的体验？

1150
00:49:18.100 --> 00:49:23.940
I had a really bad one recently because I saw, like, so I'm making this show now, this, like, stop motion show.
我最近遇到过一次特别糟糕的，因为我看到，我最近在制作这个节目，这个定格动画节目。

1151
00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:28.920
And in it, there's a, like, a lactation station, you know, that they have at the airport.
然后里面有一个，就像是，那种哺乳室，你知道的，机场都有的那种。

1152
00:49:29.140 --> 00:49:31.120
And we were getting designs for them.
然后我们当时在弄它们的设计图。

1153
00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:33.400
And I was like, oh, I want something really simple.
我就觉得，哦，我想要那种非常简单的。

1154
00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:34.960
And I was in an airport.
当时我在一个机场。

1155
00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:37.220
There was an empty lactation station.
那里有一个空着的哺乳室。

1156
00:49:37.240 --> 00:49:42.640
And I wanted to send a picture to the production designer to be like, this is what we're going for.
我想给布景设计发张照片，意思是，这就是我们想要的风格。

1157
00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:44.040
This is perfect example, right?
这真是个完美的例子，对吧？

1158
00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:51.760
So, I started to take a picture of the lactation station as someone came up to me and was like, hey, man, you were Gabe on the office.
所以，我刚开始拍那个哺乳室的照片，就有人朝我走过来，对我说，嘿，老兄，你不是《办公室》里的盖比吗。

1159
00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:55.240
And it's not lost on me that Gabe is, in many ways, quite a creepy character.
而且我心里很清楚，盖比在很多方面都是个挺让人毛骨悚然的角色。

1160
00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:01.020
And so, this guy just saw me by myself in an airport photographing a lactation station.
所以，这家伙当时就看到我一个人在机场拍哺乳室的照片。

1161
00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:01.500
Oh, no.
噢，不。

1162
00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:03.420
Like, a true predator from hell.
简直就像是个从地狱里出来的真正的掠夺者。

1163
00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:05.680
I mean, like, that's an insane thing to do, right?
我的意思是，这绝对是个疯狂的举动，对吧？

1164
00:50:05.740 --> 00:50:06.280
To take a picture.
去拍那样一张照片。

1165
00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:07.840
Like, from, he has no context.
而且，他完全没有上下文。

1166
00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:10.580
And if I say, this is for a show.
如果我说，这是为了一个节目。

1167
00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:11.720
That sounds even worse.
那听起来就更糟了。

1168
00:50:11.880 --> 00:50:12.880
It's like, it's for a show?
就好像在说，这是为了个节目？

1169
00:50:13.080 --> 00:50:13.560
Really, buddy?
真的吗，老兄？

1170
00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:21.380
Like, and so it was, that was a truly mortifying where I'm like, I, he must think I'm like, that Gabe is like a toned down version of me.
所以当时，那真是让我尴尬得要死，我在想，我，他肯定觉得我，觉得盖比其实还是我收敛之后的版本。

1171
00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:24.000
Based on, based on that experience.
根据，根据那次经历来看。

1172
00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:27.520
But, yeah, people, people sometimes will recognize me from the show.
不过，对，人们，人们有时会从这部剧里认出我来。

1173
00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:30.120
So, after the office, you did Silicon Valley.
所以，演完《办公室》之后，你演了《硅谷》。

1174
00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:31.580
You've done all these other things.
你还演了所有这些其他的作品。

1175
00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:36.900
Do you still get approached more for being on the office or for other roles?
大家找你签名合影，还是因为《办公室》更多，还是因为别的角色？

1176
00:50:36.900 --> 00:50:38.200
Or is it pretty 50-50?
还是说差不多一半一半？

1177
00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:43.160
I'd say it's like probably 50-50 with the office and then everything else I've ever done.
我可以说大概是一半一半吧，《办公室》占一半，我演过的其他所有东西占另一半。

1178
00:50:43.420 --> 00:50:46.120
Like, so it's like the single thing maybe more.
所以，就单部作品而言可能它占比更高。

1179
00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:46.980
Well, I don't know.
嗯，我不知道。

1180
00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:49.700
Silicon Valley, it's, yeah, it's probably like around 50-50.
《硅谷》，它，对，大概也是一半一半吧。

1181
00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:50.160
It depends.
这得看情况。

1182
00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:51.560
It's like demographically specific.
这有点按人群划分的。

1183
00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:52.120
Yeah.
对。

1184
00:50:52.460 --> 00:50:52.780
You know?
你知道吗？

1185
00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:53.840
And it's interesting.
而且这挺有意思的。

1186
00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:56.180
Like, if it's younger people, it's probably the office.
比如，如果是年轻人，可能就是因为《办公室》。

1187
00:50:56.560 --> 00:50:58.220
If it's like really young people.
如果是那种真的很年轻的人。

1188
00:50:58.500 --> 00:51:03.320
I think it's so sweet to me that kids find it so comforting.
我觉得小孩子们觉得这剧能带给人安慰，这一点对我来说太美好了。

1189
00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:04.820
That's a kind of emotional wallpaper.
这就好像是一种情感上的背景墙。

1190
00:51:05.060 --> 00:51:13.460
Like, people who've watched it a million times will turn it on as a way of feeling like they're kind of among their friends or in a kind of soothing, warm, silly environment.
比如，那些看过无数遍的人会把它当背景放着，好让他们觉得仿佛置身于朋友之中，或者身处一个舒缓、温暖、滑稽的环境里。

1191
00:51:13.460 --> 00:51:14.900
I think it's so sweet.
我觉得这太美好了。

1192
00:51:15.220 --> 00:51:25.420
And so it's like nice when it's like some, you know, whatever, 19-year-old who's immersed in the Scranton paper industry fictional world.
所以当看到某个，你知道的，不管是谁，19岁的年轻人沉浸在斯克兰顿造纸业的虚构世界里，那种感觉真好。

1193
00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:26.500
I think that's so sweet.
我觉得这太美好了。

1194
00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:27.480
I like that.
我很喜欢这样。

1195
00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:29.260
Have you re-watched the show?
你有重温过这部剧吗？

1196
00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:29.940
Some of it.
看过一点。

1197
00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:32.600
I hate watching anything I'm in, so not that stuff.
我讨厌看任何自己演的东西，所以那些就算了。

1198
00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:35.240
But I'd already watched the show so much when I came out.
不过在我参演之前，我就已经看过很多遍这部剧了。

1199
00:51:35.300 --> 00:51:38.060
Like, I would re-watch the show a lot before I was on the show.
比如，在加入剧组之前，我就会经常重温这部剧。

1200
00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:40.460
But I haven't done like the wall-to-wall re-watch.
但我没做过那种从头到尾的完整重温。

1201
00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:41.780
We hadn't either.
我们也没有。

1202
00:51:41.960 --> 00:51:43.400
This is the first time for us.
对我们来说这是第一次。

1203
00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:45.080
I'd seen episodes here and there.
我以前零零散散看过几集。

1204
00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:45.780
We've talked about that.
我们讨论过这个。

1205
00:51:45.780 --> 00:51:50.720
But this is really our first time to see some of these episodes in a long time since they aired.
但这是自这些剧集播出很长时间以来，我们真正第一次重新看其中一些。

1206
00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:57.480
Does it feel like, does watching the show feel like you're looking at a photo album of your friends and your life in a way?
看这部剧的感觉，会不会在某种程度上就像是在翻看你朋友和你自己生活的相册？

1207
00:51:57.540 --> 00:52:03.020
Or does it feel like you can sort of suspend disbelief and get lost in the like story of the show?
还是说你会觉得可以某种程度上暂时抛开现实，沉浸在剧集的故事中？

1208
00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:06.840
Like, are you able to invest in the fictional reality of the show when you're watching it?
也就是说，你看剧的时候能够投入到剧中的虚构现实里吗？

1209
00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:07.840
I think so.
我想是的。

1210
00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:11.300
Because of the passage of time, it's both things.
因为时间的流逝，这两种感觉都有。

1211
00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:14.060
So I have a lot of nostalgic thoughts and feelings.
所以我有很多怀旧的想法和感受。

1212
00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:16.160
I get really sentimental when I'm watching it.
看的时候我会变得非常多愁善感。

1213
00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:19.600
I remember parts of my life that were happening when we were making those episodes.
我会想起我们拍那些剧集时发生的生活片段。

1214
00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:22.700
I miss people when I watch it.
看的时候我会想念大家。

1215
00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:31.760
But I can also, when I watch it, what's weird is I have images of all the things you can't see in the episodes.
但与此同时，奇怪的是，当我看的时候，我脑海里会出现那些在剧集中看不到的幕后画面。

1216
00:52:31.760 --> 00:52:41.040
So, like, I can feel Brian Whittle holding his boom above my desk in a scene when I'm watching the rewatch.
所以，比如在我重温时的一场戏里，我能感觉到布莱恩·惠特尔把他的挑杆伸到我桌子上方。

1217
00:52:41.260 --> 00:52:45.580
You know, I can, like, feel where, oh, that video village was over in the conference room for this one.
你知道的，我能感觉到，哦，拍这一场时，监视器区域就在会议室那边。

1218
00:52:45.780 --> 00:52:47.940
And Kelly's shouting out some safety meeting.
凯莉在大声宣布什么安全会议。

1219
00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:52.420
So it's like all of the behind-the-camera stuff pops into my head as well.
所以就像所有镜头背后的东西也都涌入我的脑海里一样。

1220
00:52:52.640 --> 00:52:57.820
But then at the same time, I have found myself getting invested in the characters.
但与此同时，我也发现自己开始对角色产生代入感了。

1221
00:52:58.120 --> 00:53:03.240
Like, I personally feel that Aaron is not the one for Andy.
比如，我个人觉得艾伦根本不适合安迪。

1222
00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:04.260
Not at all.
一点也不适合。

1223
00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:05.420
And I am getting angry.
而且我开始生气了。

1224
00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:07.180
It's being shoved down my throat.
这简直是在强行灌输给我。

1225
00:53:07.180 --> 00:53:09.360
That I'm being told that they're the perfect couple.
硬跟我说他们是完美的一对。

1226
00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:12.920
When I actually feel like Andy is thriving with his current girlfriend.
而我实际上觉得安迪和现任女朋友在一起过得风生水起。

1227
00:53:13.460 --> 00:53:13.780
Jessica.
杰西卡。

1228
00:53:14.020 --> 00:53:14.340
Jessica.
杰西卡。

1229
00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:14.900
Yeah.
对。

1230
00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:15.780
Is great.
人很好。

1231
00:53:16.080 --> 00:53:16.640
I like Jessica.
我喜欢杰西卡。

1232
00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:17.200
Yeah.
对。

1233
00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:17.960
You know?
你知道吗？

1234
00:53:18.360 --> 00:53:19.640
So, I don't know.
所以，我不知道。

1235
00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:23.880
So, I do, I have, like, some fan reactions when I watch the show as well.
所以，当我看这部剧的时候，我确实也有一些粉丝般的反应。

1236
00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:25.940
What about you, Ange?
你呢，安吉？

1237
00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:28.720
Oh, I mean, I think you said that so well.
哦，我是说，你这话说的太好了。

1238
00:53:28.880 --> 00:53:30.140
That's exactly how I feel.
这正是我所想的。

1239
00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:31.540
It's both for me.
对我来说两者都有。

1240
00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:34.120
It's a photo album and I'm in the audience.
它既是一本相册，而我又像是观众。

1241
00:53:34.120 --> 00:53:35.780
And I kind of switch back and forth.
我会在两者之间来回切换。

1242
00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:42.900
I have really strong memories of episodes, you know, when things were happening in my
对于各集剧集，我有着非常深刻的记忆，你知道的，当时生活中正在发生那些事情。

1243
00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:43.360
life.
生活。

1244
00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:45.920
And I watch it with that layer.
我带着那一层记忆去看剧。

1245
00:53:46.340 --> 00:53:50.220
So, it's not even just the environment of the set, but it's the environment of your
所以，这不仅不仅仅是片场环境，更是你当时生活的环境。

1246
00:53:50.220 --> 00:53:51.260
life at that time.
生活。

1247
00:53:51.580 --> 00:53:51.860
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

1248
00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:52.820
That's fascinating.
太迷人了。

1249
00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:59.260
It was, what, nine years of our life and, you know, babies were born and family members
那是我们生活中漫长的九年，你知道的，有婴儿出生，也有家庭成员

1250
00:53:59.260 --> 00:54:00.160
passed away.
离世。

1251
00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:02.900
And it's a lot of life happens in nine years.
而且九年里发生了好多人生经历。

1252
00:54:02.900 --> 00:54:08.900
And I just watched that play out through this other filter of being a character.
我只是看着这一切通过扮演角色的另一个滤镜上演。

1253
00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:10.540
It's kind of, it's really surreal.
这感觉有点……真的非常超现实。

1254
00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:11.900
That's trippy.
这太迷幻了。

1255
00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:12.400
Yeah.
是啊。

1256
00:54:12.620 --> 00:54:15.620
A friend of mine said to me, I was, like, talking about some, maybe New York or something.
我的一个朋友对我说，我当时在谈论一些事情，也许是纽约什么的。

1257
00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:18.240
She goes, well, places aren't places as much as their times.
她说，与其说地方是地方，不如说它们是时间。

1258
00:54:18.560 --> 00:54:21.140
Like, when you think of a place, often you're thinking of a time.
就像，当你想到一个地方时，你通常想到的是一个时间。

1259
00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:21.760
Yeah.
对。

1260
00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:26.080
And so, it's not necessarily the geography of the place, but it's the sort of psychic place
所以，这不一定是那个地方的地理位置，而是那种精神上的地方

1261
00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:27.320
you were in in your life, right?
你当时生活中的状态，对吧？

1262
00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:30.180
Like, where, what was happening or who you were at that time.
就像，那个时候发生了什么，或者你是谁。

1263
00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:33.800
It's so, so interesting that it's the show where you're being somebody different.
这真的、真的很奇妙，这是一部你扮演不同角色的剧。

1264
00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:39.220
Like, you're playing a different person, but also you were a different person in your life
就像，你扮演着一个不同的人，但同时你在生活中也是一个不同的人

1265
00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:40.240
at that time, right?
在那个时候，对吧？

1266
00:54:40.300 --> 00:54:41.700
Like, the circumstances of your life were different.
就像，你生活的环境不同了。

1267
00:54:41.780 --> 00:54:42.860
Your relationships were different.
你的人际关系不同了。

1268
00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:43.480
Everything was different.
一切都不同了。

1269
00:54:43.920 --> 00:54:44.860
Can I ask just, like, one more?
我能再问一个问题吗？

1270
00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:45.640
I'm just curious.
我只是很好奇。

1271
00:54:45.740 --> 00:54:46.140
Yes, yes.
好的，好的。

1272
00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:49.700
For you guys, if there's something, like, you guys could go back and tell yourselves,
对你们来说，如果有什么事，比如，你们可以回到过去告诉自己，

1273
00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:54.440
like, in that Our Town style, like, what would you, like, if you could go back to, like,
就像《我们小镇》那种风格，你们会……如果你们能回到，比如说，

1274
00:54:54.620 --> 00:54:59.860
season one and visit with you, like, as your own, like, fairy godmother or ghost of Christmas
第一季，去看看当时的自己，就像你们自己的神仙教母或者圣诞未来幽灵

1275
00:54:59.860 --> 00:55:01.920
future or whatever, like, what would you say?
或者别的什么，你们会说些什么？

1276
00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:03.820
Or maybe that's too hard to question.
或者也许这个问题太难了。

1277
00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:04.700
Hmm.
嗯。

1278
00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:09.940
I would say I think there are a lot of ways that I know how to advocate for myself
我会说，我觉得现在我知道有很多方法来为自己

1279
00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:14.060
professionally now that I had to really learn on the go.
在职业上争取权益，而这些我以前都必须在实践中真正去学习。

1280
00:55:14.060 --> 00:55:20.060
And I feel like in some cases, I even got, like, some bad advice, you know.
我觉得在某些情况下，我甚至还得到了一些糟糕的建议，你知道的。

1281
00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:25.980
There were ways where, like, as you're kind of an up-and-coming actor and maybe you're
有些时候，作为一个冉冉升起的新星演员，也许你

1282
00:55:25.980 --> 00:55:30.960
suddenly on this hit show, there are a lot of people that have ideas for you professionally.
突然参演了这部大热剧，有很多人对你的职业生涯都有各种想法。

1283
00:55:31.740 --> 00:55:35.220
And I was just happy being on The Office.
而我当时只想开开心心待在《办公室》剧组。

1284
00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.380
I didn't need an and.
我不需要“加演”。

1285
00:55:37.600 --> 00:55:42.360
I didn't need to also have a product line or also be a movie star.
我不需要同时推出产品线，也不需要当电影明星。

1286
00:55:42.360 --> 00:55:49.020
And yet, I felt the energy coming at me, telling me I needed to do all this more.
然而，我感受到了扑面而来的压力，告诉我需要做更多这些事。

1287
00:55:49.880 --> 00:55:56.460
And I think in some ways, you know, I would do the TV show and then I would spend my hiatus
我想在某些方面，你知道的，我拍完电视剧，然后就会把假期

1288
00:55:56.460 --> 00:56:01.500
doing all the more that everyone was telling me that I wanted and that I had to do and that
用来做所有人告诉我我想要做、我必须做、而且

1289
00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:03.200
this is what you do next.
这就是你接下来该做的事。

1290
00:56:03.200 --> 00:56:10.440
And the truth is, is I just wanted to spend my hiatus traveling and enjoying my life and
但事实是，我只是想把假期花在旅行、享受生活

1291
00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:11.620
being in love.
和谈恋爱上。

1292
00:56:12.280 --> 00:56:18.160
And sometimes I look back on that time and I think, wow, I was just too busy doing a bunch
有时我回想起那段时光，心想，哇，我当时太忙了，在做一堆

1293
00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:21.060
of things that I felt like were expected of me.
我觉得别人对我的期望的事情。

1294
00:56:21.220 --> 00:56:24.660
But my truth was, I just want to be on this TV show.
但我的真实想法是，我只想参演这部电视剧。

1295
00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:25.360
I love it.
我很喜欢它。

1296
00:56:25.440 --> 00:56:25.960
I'm happy.
我很开心。

1297
00:56:25.960 --> 00:56:27.340
This is enough.
这就够了。

1298
00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:32.820
And it took me a really long time to get to a place where I didn't let other people's
我花了很长时间才达到那种状态，不再让别人的

1299
00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:35.280
idea of what my life should be.
关于我的生活应该怎样的想法左右我。

1300
00:56:35.360 --> 00:56:41.460
Like, I figured out what my ambition is in life, but I spent a lot of years.
就像，我弄清楚了自己的人生抱负是什么，但我花了很多年。

1301
00:56:41.940 --> 00:56:42.780
I don't know.
我不知道。

1302
00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:47.700
I mean, I don't necessarily regret any of those movies or any of those experiences or
我是说，我并不一定后悔拍过的那些电影、经历过的那些经历，或者

1303
00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:52.620
any of those things, but I think it's a life lesson coming to a place where you're
诸如此类的事情，但我觉得这是一个人生教训，让你意识到

1304
00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:55.300
like, well, what's enough for me is OK.
嗯，对我来说，够了就行了。

1305
00:56:55.740 --> 00:56:56.820
Yes, that's right.
是的，没错。

1306
00:56:57.420 --> 00:57:00.220
I don't have to want or need things other people need.
我不需要去渴望或需要别人需要的东西。

1307
00:57:00.380 --> 00:57:02.580
What I need is enough and that's OK.
我需要的够了就行，这就很好。

1308
00:57:02.780 --> 00:57:02.980
Yeah.
是啊。

1309
00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:10.080
And I think it's even still shocking to people when I say, no, the podcast is enough.
而且我觉得，当我拒绝时，人们依然会觉得震惊，不，播客这就够了。

1310
00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:10.960
I enjoy this.
我很享受这个。

1311
00:57:11.140 --> 00:57:13.140
I like concentrating on it.
我喜欢专注于它。

1312
00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:14.660
I like this being my one thing.
我喜欢把这当成我专心做的一件事。

1313
00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:15.580
I think maybe that's me.
我想这也许就是我吧。

1314
00:57:15.580 --> 00:57:22.400
Maybe I'm like a monogamous worker, like I have a job I like to do and that's the job
也许我像是一个工作上的“一夫一妻制”者，比如我有一份喜欢做的工作，那就是我

1315
00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:24.480
I like to do and fully and I enjoy it.
喜欢全力去做、并且很享受的工作。

1316
00:57:24.600 --> 00:57:26.060
And then I like to put it away.
然后我喜欢把它收起来。

1317
00:57:26.300 --> 00:57:28.580
Like, I don't need a lot of ands.
就像，我不需要很多个“而且”。

1318
00:57:28.740 --> 00:57:29.260
I don't.
我不需要。

1319
00:57:29.460 --> 00:57:30.620
So that's a great.
所以这真的很棒。

1320
00:57:30.840 --> 00:57:35.080
I wish I could have like told myself to trust that more in me.
我希望自己当初能告诉自己，要更相信内心的这一点。

1321
00:57:35.200 --> 00:57:38.600
It's so noisy, probably when you write like and if all of a sudden you're on the show
外界的声音太嘈杂了，也许当你写作时，或者如果突然之间你参演了一部剧

1322
00:57:38.600 --> 00:57:43.080
and it's a success and it's the cacophony of that and all of that and what and all the
而它成功了，那种喧嚣和纷扰，以及所有这一切，还有

1323
00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:44.760
projection that it cooks up and everything.
它所引发的种种投射和一切。

1324
00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:50.280
I think there's a lot of like fear based sort of communication in that, too, which
我觉得这其中也包含了很多基于恐惧的交流，比如

1325
00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:53.500
